{% set local_problem %}
<p>Guntersville's lakefront lots present unique downspout challenges. Many properties slope toward the lake — great for views, but it means water flows naturally toward the house from uphill and needs careful management to reach the lake without causing erosion.</p>

<p>Standard downspout installations often don't account for these conditions. Water gets deposited a few feet from the foundation, where it either pools (on flatter sections) or runs back toward the house (on slopes). In Guntersville's humid environment, that moisture accelerates foundation damage, wood rot, and corrosion.</p>

<p>Many lake homes also have steep roofs that shed water quickly. Standard 2x3-inch downspouts can't keep up during heavy storms, causing gutters to overflow even when clean. Overflow means uncontrolled water hitting your foundation and landscaping.</p>

<p>The solution is a downspout system designed for lake conditions: oversized spouts that handle heavy flow, positioned correctly, with extensions or underground drains that move water well away from your home.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Effective downspout systems for lake homes require three elements: capacity, positioning, and proper discharge.</p>

<p>Capacity means using downspouts large enough to handle your roof's water volume. For most Guntersville homes, that means 3x4-inch downspouts instead of standard residential sizes. Steep roofs and heavy Alabama rainfall demand adequate capacity.</p>

<p>Positioning means placing downspouts where water naturally collects and where discharge can be managed properly. On sloped lots, this requires thoughtful planning.</p>

<p>Discharge means getting water far enough from your foundation that it can't cause damage — typically through extensions or underground drains that route water to the property edge or stable drainage areas.</p>
{% endset %}

